WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.080 --> 00:00:27.120
OVERHIRING:
Almost every major tech company in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.120 --> 00:00:32.940
the world likes to claim that they operate like a&nbsp;
startup. Despite being worth hundreds of billions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.940 --> 00:00:38.700
or even trillions, these companies like to&nbsp;
claim that nothing has changed in terms of their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.700 --> 00:00:44.220
company culture relative to when they began. This&nbsp;
means that they’re still hyperfocused on growth,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.220 --> 00:00:49.680
everyone has to wear multiple hats, and most&nbsp;
importantly, they run a lean ship. Of course,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.680 --> 00:00:55.380
silicon valley startups rarely do any of this,&nbsp;
but traditionally, this is how a startup would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.380 --> 00:01:01.740
operate. Amazon is arguably the most infamous&nbsp;
company making these claims, and in one regard,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.740 --> 00:01:07.680
it’s true. Whether you’re a tech worker or&nbsp;
a factory worker, Amazon is gonna work you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:07.680 --> 00:01:13.920
to the bone just like a startup. But, unlike&nbsp;
a startup, almost all of these companies also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.920 --> 00:01:21.360
engage in overhiring. And, we’re not talking about&nbsp;
overhiring a little here or a little there. No,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:21.360 --> 00:01:27.060
we’re talking about systemic overhiring of&nbsp;
tens of thousands of people. And it’s super&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.060 --> 00:01:32.520
obvious too. Take Facebook for example.&nbsp;
At the end of 2019, they had a total of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.480 --> 00:01:39.840
45,000 employees. But, by September of 2022,&nbsp;
they had 87,000. This means that Facebook’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.840 --> 00:01:46.260
employee count nearly doubled within these three&nbsp;
years. Facebook obviously doesn’t need twice as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.260 --> 00:01:51.480
many employees to run the same services that&nbsp;
were already massive three years ago or create&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.480 --> 00:01:57.600
platforms that 38 people use. This usually isn’t&nbsp;
a big deal during bull markets when the market&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.600 --> 00:02:03.300
is doing well and investors are happy. But,&nbsp;
during recessions, companies almost instantly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.300 --> 00:02:08.340
turn around and layoff everyone that they&nbsp;
overhired. Going back to Facebook, they laid off&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.120 --> 00:02:14.520
11,000 people. Even companies that are doing&nbsp;
relatively well end up in this situation. Take&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.520 --> 00:02:19.483
Google for example. Google’s net income has&nbsp;
barely dipped and is still hovering near all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.483 --> 00:02:25.860
time highs. Yet, Google is receiving significant&nbsp;
pressure to reduce their workforce, and there are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.860 --> 00:02:30.840
rumors that Google is looking to layoff 10,000&nbsp;
people. But, why even play this entire game?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.840 --> 00:02:38.040
Why hire to fire? Wouldn’t it make sense to run a&nbsp;
lean ship at all times like a startup? This way,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.040 --> 00:02:43.500
during good times, their profit margins will be&nbsp;
even higher, and during bad times, they won’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.500 --> 00:02:49.980
have to shed too much of their workforce if any. I&nbsp;
mean, it seems pretty straightforward, yet most of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.980 --> 00:02:55.980
these companies still overhire. So, here’s why&nbsp;
companies overhire just to end up laying off.

00:02:55.980 --> 00:02:58.200
FOMO:
Much of these corporations&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.200 --> 00:03:07.680
hiring practices can be linked back to&nbsp;
one simple human emotion: fear, the fear&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.680 --> 00:03:12.780
of missing out. During periods of hypergrowth,&nbsp;
it seems like everyone around you is investing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.780 --> 00:03:18.420
and growing way faster than you. This was very&nbsp;
much true for Amazon. From the pandemic bottom,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.420 --> 00:03:25.620
Amazon stock grew about 132% which is a great&nbsp;
number. But, from Amazon’s perspective, many of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.620 --> 00:03:33.240
their peers grew substantially more. Facebook&nbsp;
grew 180%, Google grew 200%, Apple grew 240%,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.240 --> 00:03:41.640
and heck even the S&amp;P 500 grew 120%. Looking at&nbsp;
it like this, Amazon barely beat the market and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:41.640 --> 00:03:46.380
they significantly underperformed their peers.&nbsp;
Now, the smart thing to do would be to identify&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.380 --> 00:03:52.200
areas in which Amazon could fundamentally grow.&nbsp;
But, oftentimes, what happens is they look at what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.200 --> 00:03:57.480
their peers are doing. And more times than not,&nbsp;
their peers are pouring crap loads of money into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:57.480 --> 00:04:03.540
research and development. For example, Facebook&nbsp;
was pouring a $100 billion into the Metaverse.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:03.540 --> 00:04:10.740
And when Amazon sees this, they think: Ah, that&nbsp;
must be the key. If we want to grow at the pace of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.740 --> 00:04:17.520
Facebook, we have to double down on R&amp;D. And this&nbsp;
is how you end up with projects like Alexa that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:17.520 --> 00:04:22.740
are massive money pits. During bull markets, it’s&nbsp;
easy to say that these investments are for the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:22.740 --> 00:04:27.780
long haul, but when things turn around, it doesn’t&nbsp;
take long for companies to get a reality check.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:27.780 --> 00:04:34.020
Aside from overinvesting into R&amp;D, companies&nbsp;
also end up overinvesting into marketing.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:34.020 --> 00:04:39.240
When companies realize that they're falling behind&nbsp;
their competitors, a natural response is to become&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:39.240 --> 00:04:44.520
more aggressive with sales. This includes hiring&nbsp;
a bunch of sales people, pumping up ad budgets,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:44.520 --> 00:04:50.100
and creating overly generous referral programs.&nbsp;
This overinvestment is only made worse by the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:50.100 --> 00:04:54.660
positive feedback loops that follow. As&nbsp;
companies pump up marketing, it’ll actually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.660 --> 00:04:59.700
appear like the investment is worth it. They’ll&nbsp;
have great click through rates, conversion rates,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.700 --> 00:05:05.580
and customer acquisition costs, so they’ll end up&nbsp;
spending even more on marketing. They’ll also hire&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:05.580 --> 00:05:10.800
a bunch of new developers, solutions architects,&nbsp;
and managers to keep up with this demand. But,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:10.800 --> 00:05:16.080
in reality, much of this growth can be explained&nbsp;
by consumer sentiment. As you’re probably aware,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:16.080 --> 00:05:20.520
the bull market FOMO doesn’t just affect&nbsp;
companies, it also affects the general&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:20.520 --> 00:05:25.980
public. When you see your friends and family buy&nbsp;
new cars and houses, you naturally feel a sense&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:25.980 --> 00:05:32.640
of security and safety as it feels like everything&nbsp;
goes well. This doesn’t mean that you go out and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:32.640 --> 00:05:38.520
buy a car yourself, but maybe, you might be a bit&nbsp;
more loose when it comes to your Amazon spending&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:38.520 --> 00:05:43.080
or you might keep around subscriptions that&nbsp;
you don’t actually use. But, when a recession&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:43.080 --> 00:05:47.220
rolls around and you see your friends and family&nbsp;
losing their jobs and struggling to make payments,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:47.220 --> 00:05:52.740
you’re naturally going to put off that iPhone&nbsp;
upgrade and eat out less. When companies see this,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:52.740 --> 00:05:57.120
it’ll appear as if their sales team is no&nbsp;
longer as effective. So, they’ll end up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:57.120 --> 00:06:01.380
scaling back their sales team which in turn&nbsp;
means scaling back the rest of the company.

00:06:05.580 --> 00:06:08.460
DEFENSIVE:
It may not seem like it, but overhiring&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:08.460 --> 00:06:14.280
is actually oftentimes a defensive move. Here’s&nbsp;
the thing, when things are going well, employees&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:14.280 --> 00:06:18.960
are usually pretty confident. They’ll be willing&nbsp;
to ask for promotions, look for other jobs,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:18.960 --> 00:06:24.060
and join riskier organizations. And given that&nbsp;
everyone is chucking a bunch of money into sales&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:24.060 --> 00:06:30.240
and R&amp;D, it’s usually not that difficult for these&nbsp;
employees to find a better job. All of a sudden,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:30.240 --> 00:06:35.940
getting into the most prestigious companies won’t&nbsp;
be as impossible as it once seemed. And joining a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:35.940 --> 00:06:41.880
crypto brokerage won’t seem as irresponsible as&nbsp;
it once seemed. Also, we’re not talking about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:41.880 --> 00:06:47.580
nominal increases to your salary like going from&nbsp;
$60,000 to $70,000. We’re talking about jumps like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:47.580 --> 00:06:55.200
$70,000 to $120,000 or $150,000 to $280,000. I&nbsp;
mean, you just have to take one look at these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:55.200 --> 00:06:59.460
companies' salaries to understand just how much&nbsp;
their willing to pay to attract the best talent.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:59.460 --> 00:07:04.560
At the same time, even employees that don’t leave&nbsp;
will be more confident in setting boundaries.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:04.560 --> 00:07:09.360
While these guys ended up sticking around, they’re&nbsp;
usually well aware that they could switch just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:09.360 --> 00:07:14.280
like their peers. They probably also see a bunch&nbsp;
of vacancies around them which means that the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:14.280 --> 00:07:18.900
company is struggling to hire enough talent. All&nbsp;
of this shows that the employer is more dependent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:18.900 --> 00:07:24.420
on the employee than the employee is dependent&nbsp;
on the employer. This means that the employee is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:24.420 --> 00:07:29.940
unlikely to work outside of their regular hours&nbsp;
or take on work that’s outside their scope. This&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:29.940 --> 00:07:35.160
is pretty much exactly what we were seeing till&nbsp;
recently. Everyone was resigning and switching&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:35.160 --> 00:07:41.220
jobs or they were quiet quitting. Companies are&nbsp;
acutely aware of this, but they really can’t do&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:41.220 --> 00:07:45.840
much about it. If they force their employees to&nbsp;
be more productive, more of them will probably&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:45.840 --> 00:07:51.240
leave which will just mean that the company has&nbsp;
even more positions to fill likely at higher&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:51.240 --> 00:07:56.160
salaries as well. To combat this, companies have&nbsp;
to overhire to account for the lower productivity&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:56.160 --> 00:08:02.280
and higher churn. This was the case just one&nbsp;
year ago. At the end of 2021, Mark Zuckerberg was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:02.280 --> 00:08:06.540
irritated that Facebook wasn’t able to meet their&nbsp;
recruiting targets especially with experienced&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:06.540 --> 00:08:11.880
engineers. In fact, Facebook was struggling to&nbsp;
get even half of their applicants to accept job&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:11.880 --> 00:08:17.280
offers despite literally offering millions.&nbsp;
But, this entire dynamic turns on its head&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:17.280 --> 00:08:23.220
during a recessionary period. For one, employees&nbsp;
are not only less likely to leave but they’re also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:23.220 --> 00:08:28.380
more likely to be productive because they wanna&nbsp;
keep their jobs. This suddenly means that you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:28.380 --> 00:08:34.200
can accomplish all of the same tasks with just&nbsp;
70 or 80% of your workforce. At the same time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:34.200 --> 00:08:39.660
new hires start piling up. Before, you had to&nbsp;
recruit like crazy just to maintain your headcount&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:39.660 --> 00:08:44.640
and account for the massive churn. But now, all&nbsp;
of these new hires quickly become a deadweight for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:44.640 --> 00:08:50.340
the company. For example, let’s say the company&nbsp;
was overhiring by 10%. If their existing staff&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:50.340 --> 00:08:56.640
suddenly becomes 30% more productive, the company&nbsp;
is no longer overstaffed by just 10%, they’re&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:56.640 --> 00:09:02.280
overstaffed by 40%. And, at this point, there’s&nbsp;
nothing left to do but bring out the chopper.

00:09:03.900 --> 00:09:07.560
HIRE TO FIRE:
The points that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:07.560 --> 00:09:13.740
we’ve discussed so far are pretty understandable.&nbsp;
During bull markets, companies are often fearful&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:13.740 --> 00:09:19.140
of falling behind and they’re forced to overhire&nbsp;
just to keep up. When market dynamics shift,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:19.140 --> 00:09:25.380
however, this often results in massive layoffs.&nbsp;
While this no doubt sucks, it’s atleast somewhat&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:25.380 --> 00:09:30.060
understandable. What’s not understandable,&nbsp;
however, is the third subset of reasons&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:30.060 --> 00:09:36.060
that companies overhire which is the nefarious&nbsp;
subset. One of the most nefarious of these is the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:36.060 --> 00:09:41.940
practice of hiring to fire. At first glance,&nbsp;
this probably sounds super counterintuitive,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:41.940 --> 00:09:48.840
and it is. Why would anyone spend so much time,&nbsp;
money, and effort into vetting and interviewing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:48.840 --> 00:09:54.600
thousands of applicants just to turn around&nbsp;
and fire them? Well, let’s just say, it’s not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:54.600 --> 00:10:00.000
something that anyone really wants to do, but&nbsp;
certain corporate policies make this a reality.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:00.000 --> 00:10:06.120
For example, Amazon requires that the bottom 10%&nbsp;
of their workforce be let go on an annual basis.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:06.120 --> 00:10:11.760
If you’re an Amazon manager, this would be easy to&nbsp;
do if you have a crappy worker on your team. But,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:11.760 --> 00:10:17.040
unfortunately, most Amazon managers aren’t that&nbsp;
lucky given that everyone that they hire for the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:17.040 --> 00:10:22.140
corporate side are exceptional. If they were to&nbsp;
score their team members, the worst performer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:22.140 --> 00:10:29.400
might be given a 90%. Obviously, there’s some room&nbsp;
for improvement here, but it’s definitely not a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:29.400 --> 00:10:35.640
fireable offense. Managers very much understand&nbsp;
that this would be unfair and that’s why they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:35.640 --> 00:10:41.220
turn to hiring to fire. They usually maintain&nbsp;
a core team of 7 to 10 people that they want&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:41.220 --> 00:10:45.900
to keep permanently. And every year, they’ll&nbsp;
hire some random sucker who will be tasked&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:45.900 --> 00:10:50.640
with doing all the dirty work for the team for&nbsp;
a year before getting fired. If a given sucker&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:50.640 --> 00:10:56.340
truly does an exceptional job, there is a chance&nbsp;
that they’ll be allowed to stick around, but this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:56.340 --> 00:11:02.280
means that someone else on the team now has to&nbsp;
be fired instead and this is by design. A lot of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:02.280 --> 00:11:08.520
companies don’t care about tenure or experience.&nbsp;
In fact, they feel like these attributes make an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:08.520 --> 00:11:13.080
employee less productive because they become too&nbsp;
comfortable. These companies would much rather&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:13.080 --> 00:11:17.340
have a bunch of short term employees that work&nbsp;
their butts off the entire time they’re there.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:17.340 --> 00:11:24.180
And this forces managers to either hire to fire&nbsp;
or hire to replace. It’s truly quite a vicious&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:24.180 --> 00:11:30.000
practice, but it’s not just Amazon who’s guilty&nbsp;
of this. Most old school corporate giants practice&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:30.000 --> 00:11:38.160
some form of this. Think companies like GE, IBM,&nbsp;
and even Microsoft up until 2013. We should note,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:38.160 --> 00:11:44.520
however, that it wasn’t till after 2013 that&nbsp;
Microsoft finally started to grow again. So, this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:44.520 --> 00:11:50.580
practice is definitely just as counterproductive&nbsp;
as it sounds. But, nonetheless, many companies&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:50.580 --> 00:11:55.320
still practice it leading to constant&nbsp;
overhiring followed by layoffs and firing.

00:11:55.320 --> 00:12:02.460
WHY COMPANIES OVERHIRE:
In the end, while it might be easy to say that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:02.460 --> 00:12:07.740
companies should just hire what they need, it’s&nbsp;
usually not that simple. The underlying problem&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:07.740 --> 00:12:22.860
is that the number of employees that a company&nbsp;
needs is simply not constant. When everyone is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:22.860 --> 00:12:28.620
spending and buying, companies naturally need&nbsp;
more employees. Conservely, when everyone is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:28.620 --> 00:12:33.720
pulling back, companies need less employees.&nbsp;
Combine this with varying levels of productivity&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:33.720 --> 00:12:40.140
and opportunities and this issue just becomes&nbsp;
even more magnified. But, this doesn’t mean that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:40.140 --> 00:12:45.600
companies are completely innocent either. Many of&nbsp;
them engage in terrible practices like hiring to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:45.600 --> 00:12:52.200
fire or hiring to replace which not only increases&nbsp;
churn, but also creates a horrendous workplace&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:52.200 --> 00:12:57.840
culture which makes the entire organization less&nbsp;
productive. Really, the only way to avoid this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:57.840 --> 00:13:03.600
is to join some sort background company like an&nbsp;
energy company or a water company or of course&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:03.600 --> 00:13:09.060
the government. But for the rest of us, well,&nbsp;
we’ll probably have to deal with these corporate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:09.060 --> 00:13:13.920
games throughout the rest of our careers, but&nbsp;
that’s just what I think. What are your thoughts&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:13.920 --> 00:13:19.560
on hiring to fire? Comment that down below. Also,&nbsp;
drop a like if you’re hoping that the layoffs end&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:19.560 --> 00:13:24.120
sooner rather than later. And of course consider&nbsp;
checking out our discord community to suggest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:24.120 --> 00:13:27.840
future video ideas and consider subscribing&nbsp;
to see more questions logically answered.

